# Types

Illegal states are types. They are not a rustdoc note, not a README caveat, and not an error returned when two options are combined.

The concrete types and error variants live in `docs/design.ja.md`. Do not copy them here.

## Runtime validation is the boundary only

A check that returns `Result` is allowed where a value enters the type system: a public entry point that receives raw coordinates, a length, or a dimension from the caller. Past that boundary, code trusts the type.

A lifecycle precondition is not an exception. Split the type. Do not keep a flag and reject the wrong state at runtime.

```rust
// BAD
struct Hull {
    built: bool,
}

// GOOD
struct HullBuilder { /* accepts points */ }
struct ConvexHull { /* only exists after a successful build */ }
```

## Do not ship

- A field that is ignored when another field is set
- "If both are set, return an error"
- A flag that turns the rest of the struct off
- A public method that panics because the caller used the wrong phase of the same type

Input rejection the design already names (non-finite coordinates, the wrong length, an empty dimension) stays a boundary `Result`. That is not a license to encode mutually exclusive modes as booleans.
